# Garage — example environment for the containerised stack.
#
#   cp .env.docker.example .env.docker
#   docker compose --env-file .env.docker --profile dev --profile app up -d --build
#
# This file exists because `.env` describes a different topology, not a
# different application. On the host the API is at `localhost:3000` and
# Postgres at `localhost:5432`; inside the compose network they are at
# `garage-api:3000` and `postgres:5432`. Same code, same schemas
# (`apps/garage/api/src/env.ts`, `apps/garage/web/src/env.ts`), different addresses.
#
# Every value below is an obviously-fake dev value. NEVER put a real secret in
# this file — it is committed. `.env.docker` (without `.example`) is
# git-ignored and is where your own values go; in a real deployment they come
# from the platform's secret store and this file is not used at all.
#
# One host-side prerequisite, once, for the mock OIDC path only. Add to
# /etc/hosts:
#
#     127.0.0.1 garage-api garage-oidc
#
# Both names have to mean the same server from the browser and from inside the
# containers: the browser is redirected to the issuer, and the web container
# calls both the issuer and the API server-side. Docker's DNS covers the
# inside; this line covers the browser. With a real Okta tenant the issuer is
# already a public name and only `garage-api` is needed — or neither, if
# the API is published under a real hostname too.

# --- infrastructure ---------------------------------------------------------

# Credentials docker compose gives the `postgres` container. Keep in sync with
# DATABASE_URL below by hand — the two are not derived from each other.
POSTGRES_USER=garage
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=garage_dev_password
POSTGRES_DB=garage

# --- apps/garage/api ---------------------------------------------------------------

# `postgres`, not `localhost`: the container's own loopback is the container.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://garage:garage_dev_password@postgres:5432/garage

# The mock issuer's network alias (docker-compose.yml, §mock-oauth2-server).
# In production: your Okta **Custom** Authorization Server, e.g.
# https://<tenant>.okta.com/oauth2/default — an Org AS lets sign-in succeed
# and then 401s every API call. See README.md, "Okta".
AUTH_OKTA_ISSUER=http://garage-oidc:8080/default

# `default` is what mock-oauth2-server mints with no JSON_CONFIG. Against a
# real Custom AS this is that server's audience, typically `api://default`.
# See .env.example for the full derivation.
AUTH_OKTA_AUDIENCE=default

# The **browser's** origin, which is the host port `web` is published on — not
# the container's. No wildcard; absolute URLs only.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:4200

LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- apps/garage/web ---------------------------------------------------------------

# The API's base URL, global prefix included. Read by server components and by
# `/api/health` at request time, then handed to the browser — so it must
# resolve from both. Hence `garage-api` and the /etc/hosts line above.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://garage-api:3000/api

# The origin a browser reaches this app on — the published host port, not the
# container's. Auth.js redirects here after a successful sign-in; without it
# the redirect goes to the address the server bound (`0.0.0.0:3000`) and the
# browser fails to load a page it has already been authenticated for. Not
# needed for host-run dev, where the two happen to be the same address.
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:4200

# Auth.js session cookie secret, at least 32 characters. Generate your own:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
AUTH_SECRET=dev-secret-please-regenerate-with-opensslrandhex32-not-real

# mock-oauth2-server accepts any client_id/secret, so these fake values work.
AUTH_OKTA_CLIENT_ID=garage-web-dev
AUTH_OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET=garage-web-dev-secret

# --- Slack notifications (apps/garage/api) -----------------------------------------
#
# `docker-compose.yml` passes the whole `SLACK_*` block through to the `api`
# service as bare entries, so an unset name is simply not forwarded
# (`doc/decision/0205-the-app-profile-names-every-variable-it-passes`). This
# block exists so the containerised stack says the same thing `.env.example`
# does about a variable that is a live outbound integration.
#
# Off, and that is the only thing between a container on your laptop and a real
# workspace: there is no `NODE_ENV` branch and no "pretend to send" mode
# (`doc/decision/0130-slack-is-off-by-default-and-there-is-no-dev-only-branch`).
# Only `true` and `false` parse. With it off every notification still builds its
# message; the call stops at the one gate in `SlackClient`.
SLACK_ENABLED=false

# **`SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` is a secret and MUST NOT be written here.** This file is
# committed. Set it in `.env.docker` (git-ignored) or, in a real deployment,
# from the platform's secret store. Both are optional on their own, and a
# refinement in `apps/garage/api/src/env.ts` makes them **required when
# SLACK_ENABLED=true** — so "enabled but unconfigured" is a boot crash naming
# the variable, not a stream of runtime errors.
#
# Bot token (`xoxb-…`) with `chat:write` and `users:read.email`:
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=
#
# Channel the freed-spot notice and the daily summary are posted to. The bot
# must be a member of it, or Slack answers `not_in_channel`:
# SLACK_CHANNEL_ID=

# Per-attempt HTTP timeout, total attempts (the first included), the first
# backoff delay (it doubles per further attempt), and when the daily summary is
# posted — `HH:MM` **in Europe/Prague**. Defaults shown; see `.env.example`.
# SLACK_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
# SLACK_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=3
# SLACK_RETRY_BASE_DELAY_MS=500
# SLACK_DAILY_SUMMARY_AT=08:00
